course: dictionary
lesson: 9825
title: One word, many meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Some words change their meaning depending on who is speaking and where. «Sweetie» is one of them. Read the words around it to find out which meaning fits. «Thank you, sweetie» is said warmly, so it is a loving name you call someone you like.
explain: «She's a real sweetie» means she is a kind, lovely person. The clue is the word real: it shows praise for her kindness, not a sweet to eat.
explain: In the UK, especially Scotland, a child might ask for «sweeties», meaning sweets. When you are not sure, put each meaning into the sentence and see which one still makes sense.
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Sweetie» can mean a loving name you call someone you like, such as a child or a partner.
fact: «Sweetie» can also mean a kind, lovely person.
fact: In the UK, especially Scotland, a child's word «sweetie» means a sweet, a piece of candy.
fact: In Scots, «pure sound» means really nice, and «nippy sweetie» is Scots slang for a sharp-tongued person, which is not in this dictionary entry.
exercise: «Thank you, sweetie.» What does sweetie mean here? | *A loving name you call someone you like | A small dog | A bag of sugar | A cold drink
exercise: «She's a real sweetie.» What does sweetie mean? | A sweet to eat | *A kind, lovely person | A loud shout | A piece of cake
exercise: In Scotland, a child asks for «sweeties». What does the child want? | Love letters | Sweethearts to marry | Honey for toast | *Sweets, pieces of candy
test: «Nan calls me sweetie when I visit.» What does sweetie mean here? | A bowl of sugar | A type of bread | *A loving name for someone she likes | A kind of bus
test: «Can I have a sweetie, please, Mum?» In the UK, what is a sweetie? | A kind, lovely person | A birthday present | A cup of tea | *A sweet, a piece of candy
exam: «The shop sells sweeties for a penny.» What does sweeties mean here? | A cold drink | A loving name for a friend | A kind, lovely person | *Sweets, pieces of candy
